    {
      "check_id": "CHK-0001",
      "property_id": "PROP-0036",
      "title": "Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Is data column sidecar publishing restricted to subnets the node is subscribed to via custody or sampling duties?",
      "severity": "Medium",
      "mindset": "Boundary Guard",
      "is_boundary_check": true,
      "reachability": {
        "classification": "external-reachable",
        "entry_points": ["P2P"],
        "attacker_controlled": false,
        "validation_layers": ["Subscription check"],
        "bug_bounty_scope": "in-scope",
        "notes": "Publishing to non-subscribed subnets is unauthorized."
      },
      "test_procedure": "1. Identify the function responsible for data column sidecar publishing (distribute_blob_sidecars or equivalent in client implementation). 2. Trace the logic that determines which subnets the node is subscribed to, verifying it calls get_custody_columns or equivalent to retrieve the node's assigned custody/sampling subnets. 3. Verify that before publishing a sidecar to a given subnet_id, the code checks whether subnet_id is in the set of subnets the node is subscribed to (derived from custody groups and sampling duties). 4. Attempt to construct a scenario where a sidecar is published to a subnet_id NOT in the node's custody or sampling column set. This could occur via: (a) a race condition where custody assignments change during publishing, (b) incorrect mapping from column_index to subnet_id via compute_subnet_for_data_column_sidecar, or (c) the publish function iterating over all columns rather than only custody/sampling columns. 5. Verify that the subscription check is performed at the gossip publish layer, not just at the application layer, ensuring the underlying libp2p gossipsub implementation also does not leak messages to non-subscribed topics. 6. Check for edge cases at epoch boundaries where custody assignments may rotate, verifying that publishing uses the correct epoch's custody assignment.",
      "bug_class": "Authorization Bypass",
      "risk_category": "Elevation of Privilege",
      "code_locations": [
        {
          "file": "consensus-specs/specs/fulu/p2p-interface.md",
          "function": "distribute_blob_sidecars",
          "notes": "The function responsible for distributing data column sidecars to gossip subnets. Must filter columns to only those matching the node's custody or sampling duties."
        },
        {
          "file": "consensus-specs/specs/fulu/das-core.md",
          "function": "get_custody_columns",
          "notes": "Determines which columns a node is responsible for based on its node_id and custody group assignments."
        },
        {
          "file": "consensus-specs/specs/fulu/p2p-interface.md",
          "function": "compute_subnet_for_data_column_sidecar",
          "notes": "Maps column_index to subnet_id. Correctness of this mapping is essential for publishing to the correct subnet."
        }
      ],
      "executable_checks": [
        "Unit test: Verify distribute_blob_sidecars only publishes to subnets matching get_custody_columns output for the node",
        "Unit test: Verify that sidecars for columns NOT in the node's custody set are not published to any gossip topic",
        "Integration test: Run a node with known custody assignments and verify via gossip message tracing that no sidecar is published to a non-custody subnet",
        "Edge case test: Verify correct behavior during custody rotation at epoch boundaries, ensuring no stale custody assignment causes publishing to wrong subnets",
        "Property-based test: forall (column_index, subnet_id) pairs published, subnet_id in node_custody_subnets(current_epoch)"
      ],
      "notes": "Source: PROP-0036, Trust Boundary: tb-005 (beacon-node to gossip-network). This is a boundary property at the edge where the local beacon node publishes data to the external gossip network. While the attacker does not directly control this (attacker_controlled=false), a bug here could cause unauthorized data propagation, subnet pollution, or violation of custody invariants that PeerDAS data availability relies upon. The severity is Medium because the primary impact is unauthorized influence on data availability beyond the node's designated role, rather than direct data corruption."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "CHK-0002",
      "property_id": "PROP-0036",
      "title": "Supporting Node Check: Does the gossip network layer enforce topic subscription boundaries for outgoing sidecar messages?",
      "severity": "Medium",
      "mindset": "Boundary Guard",
      "is_boundary_check": false,
      "reachability": {
        "classification": "external-reachable",
        "entry_points": ["P2P"],
        "attacker_controlled": false,
        "validation_layers": ["Subscription check"],
        "bug_bounty_scope": "in-scope",
        "notes": "Publishing to non-subscribed subnets is unauthorized."
      },
      "test_procedure": "1. Review the gossipsub implementation layer (libp2p) to verify that publish operations are restricted to topics the node is currently subscribed to. 2. Verify that the beacon node's gossip topic management correctly subscribes and unsubscribes from data_column_sidecar_{subnet_id} topics as custody assignments change. 3. Check whether a message can be published to a gossipsub topic the node is not subscribed to (some implementations may allow this). 4. If the underlying gossipsub allows publishing to unsubscribed topics, verify that the application layer (beacon node) has an explicit guard preventing this. 5. Verify that when a node transitions between epochs and its custody columns change, the topic subscriptions are updated atomically or with proper ordering (unsubscribe old before publishing on new, or subscribe new before unsubscribing old).",
      "bug_class": "Authorization Bypass",
      "risk_category": "Elevation of Privilege",
      "code_locations": [
        {
          "file": "Client-specific gossipsub implementation",
          "function": "publish / subscribe / unsubscribe topic handlers",
          "notes": "The gossipsub layer that handles topic management and message publishing. Implementation varies by client (Lighthouse uses rust-libp2p, Prysm uses go-libp2p, etc.)."
        },
        {
          "file": "consensus-specs/specs/fulu/p2p-interface.md",
          "function": "Topic subscription management for data_column_sidecar_{subnet_id}",
          "notes": "The specification defines which subnets a node should subscribe to based on custody requirements."
        }
      ],
      "executable_checks": [
        "Unit test: Attempt to call gossip publish on a topic the node is not subscribed to and verify it is rejected or silently dropped",
        "Integration test: Monitor gossip messages during custody rotation and verify no message leaks to old/wrong subnet topics",
        "Negative test: Configure a node with CUSTODY_REQUIREMENT=4 subnets and verify zero messages are published to the remaining 124 subnets"
      ],
      "notes": "Source: PROP-0036, Supporting check for actor-gossip-network node. This verifies the gossip network layer's role in enforcing subnet boundaries. Even if the beacon node application layer correctly filters, a permissive gossipsub implementation could still leak messages."
